          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "Roots solve \\(a(x-h)^2+k=0\\), i.e. \\(x=h\\pm\\sqrt{-k/a}\\) — the vertex-form shortcut to the quadratic formula above.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Quadratic formula",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Vertex",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Difference of squares",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( a^2-b^2=(a+b)(a-b) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Perfect square",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( (a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2 \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Solve \\( x^2-5x+6=0 \\) by factoring. \\[ (x-2)(x-3)=0 \\implies x=2 \\text{ or } x=3. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "<h3>Polynomial operations</h3>To multiply polynomials, distribute each term of the first factor across every term of the second. The key identity \\( (a+b)(a-b)=a^2-b^2 \\) shortcuts many EST questions. Dividing a polynomial by a linear factor \\( (x-k) \\) uses synthetic or long division; the Remainder Theorem states the remainder equals \\( f(k) \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — Discriminant & nature of roots",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Find the values of \\(k\\) for which \\(x^2 + kx + 9 = 0\\) has two equal roots. <p>Equal roots when \\(\\Delta = 0\\): \\(k^2 - 4(1)(9) = 0 \\Rightarrow k^2 = 36 \\Rightarrow k = \\pm 6\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Solve \\(2x^2 - 7x + 3 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(x = \\dfrac{7 \\pm \\sqrt{49-24}}{4} = \\dfrac{7 \\pm 5}{4}\\). So \\(x = 3\\) or \\(x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
